WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.589
Because of the ship’s tilt, the bay was below me; Israel Hands had fallen between me and the rest of the ship .

2
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:13.457
He rose to the surface in a lather of foam and blood, and then bobbed in the waves.

3
00:00:13.458 --> 00:00:23.583
I felt sick, faint, and terrified. The dagger burned hot in my shoulder, and I clung to the ropes with both hands until my fingernails ached.

4
00:00:23.584 --> 00:00:31.815
I wanted to be free from the mast, but I thought falling into the water beside the dead body of Israel Hands would be far worse!

5
00:00:31.816 --> 00:00:35.137
I could feel hot blood running over my back and chest.

6
00:00:35.138 --> 00:00:41.771
"Pull out the knife!" I told myself. But neither of my hands would let go of the ropes.

7
00:00:41.772 --> 00:00:46.804
I started to cry and shuddered at the thought of my predicament.

8
00:00:46.805 --> 00:00:56.108
Oddly enough, my shuddering helped. The knife, which I thought had stuck me to the mast so securely, had really almost missed me completely.

9
00:00:56.109 --> 00:01:01.945
I was held to the mast only by a mere pinch of skin, and my shudder tore it away.

10
00:01:01.946 --> 00:01:06.031
Blood ran down my back faster, but I was free.

11
00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:13.341
I pulled my coat and shirt away, moved to the opposite side of the ship, and climbed down on that side.

12
00:01:13.342 --> 00:01:17.522
Nothing could make me go down the ropes above where Israel had fallen.

13
00:01:17.523 --> 00:01:21.379
I went below deck and did what I could for my wound.

14
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:28.481
I was in pain, but the wound was neither deep nor dangerous, nor did it bother me too much to use my arm.

15
00:01:28.482 --> 00:01:32.596
Back up on the top deck, I looked around and saw Red Cap.

16
00:01:32.597 --> 00:01:38.005
He was leaning against the side of the ship like some horrible, life-size puppet.

17
00:01:38.006 --> 00:01:42.281
"He looks easier to move now," I said to myself.

18
00:01:42.282 --> 00:01:50.807
After what I had been through, another dead body didn’t bother me, so I grabbed his belt and, with one good heave, threw him overboard.

19
00:01:50.808 --> 00:01:56.745
He went in with a splash, but his red cap came off and remained floating in the water.

20
00:01:56.746 --> 00:02:02.567
The evening breeze started whistling softly through the rope lines and rattled the sails.

21
00:02:02.568 --> 00:02:06.242
"The wind may be a danger to the ship," I thought.

22
00:02:06.243 --> 00:02:11.509
I was able to get the front sails down, but the mainsail was too difficult to manage.

23
00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:17.922
When the ship tipped, the mainsail had swung out so that now a foot or two of the sail was under water.

24
00:02:17.923 --> 00:02:25.495
I got my knife and cut the lines that usually raise and lower the sail, and the loose canvas dropped instantly into the water.

25
00:02:25.496 --> 00:02:31.012
"Now both the Hispaniola and I must hope for a little luck," I said.

26
00:02:31.013 --> 00:02:38.052
I suddenly realized the sun had set and the air was getting chilly, so I let myself drop softly overboard. The

27
00:02:38.053 --> 00:02:44.253
water barely reached my waist, and I waded ashore as the breeze whistled low among the pines. I

28
00:02:44.254 --> 00:02:49.456
wanted to get to my friends and boast that I had recaptured the ship single-handedly. "Even

29
00:02:49.457 --> 00:02:53.683
Captain Smollett will admit I’m resourceful and brave," I thought. "He

30
00:02:53.684 --> 00:03:00.084
may even want to give me a medal or something!" I laughed out loud at that. In

31
00:03:00.085 --> 00:03:07.602
high spirits, I set out for the stockade, and before long, I was near the hill where I’d first encountered Ben Gunn. In

32
00:03:07.603 --> 00:03:13.820
the distance, I could see a glow against the sky and thought Ben Gunn was cooking his supper. "Why

33
00:03:13.821 --> 00:03:20.414
is he so careless?" I wondered. "If I can see his fire, so can Long John." Gradually

34
00:03:20.415 --> 00:03:22.798
the night became darker. I

35
00:03:22.799 --> 00:03:29.780
kept tripping over tree roots and stumbling into sandpits until the moon finally rose and gave me some light. I

36
00:03:29.781 --> 00:03:34.402
continued walking, sometimes running impatiently, to the stockade. "I

37
00:03:34.403 --> 00:03:40.764
must stay alert," I thought. "I don’t want my friends to shoot me by mistake." As

38
00:03:40.765 --> 00:03:45.543
the moon climbed higher, the glow appeared among the trees again. This

39
00:03:45.544 --> 00:03:52.288
time, the fire resembled the red-hot embers of a smoldering bonfire somewhere near the stockade. By

40
00:03:52.289 --> 00:04:00.140
the time I reached the fence, I saw the remains of what must have been an immense fire at the side of the house. "Did

41
00:04:00.141 --> 00:04:03.776
something go wrong while I was absent?" I wondered. I

42
00:04:03.777 --> 00:04:10.323
climbed the fence, got on my hands and knees, and crawled without a sound toward the corner of the house. As

43
00:04:10.324 --> 00:04:19.119
I drew near, I heard my friends snoring, peaceful in their sleep, and the unpleasant noises sounded like music to me. "If

44
00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:26.087
Long John was creeping in on them like this, my friends would all be dead by daybreak," I said to myself. "I

45
00:04:26.088 --> 00:04:30.802
shouldn’t have left them with so few men remaining to guard the house." I

46
00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:31.986
crept in. "I

47
00:04:31.987 --> 00:04:40.033
should lie down in my usual place," I thought with a chuckle, "and enjoy the looks on their faces when they find me in the morning." My

48
00:04:40.034 --> 00:04:45.043
foot struck a sleeper’s leg, and he turned and groaned, but didn’t wake. And

49
00:04:45.044 --> 00:04:50.487
then suddenly a shrill voice screamed from the darkness. "Pieces

50
00:04:50.488 --> 00:04:55.399
of eight! Pieces of eight! Pieces of eight!" "Long

51
00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:03.287
John’s parrot!" I whispered. My body froze with fear as the sleepers awoke and jumped up. "Who

52
00:05:03.288 --> 00:05:07.248
goes there?" roared Long John Silver. I

53
00:05:07.249 --> 00:05:14.028
turned to run, slammed violently against one person, jumped back, and ran into the arms o f a second man.

54
00:05:14.029 --> 00:05:17.556
He held me tight as I struggled against his grip.

55
00:05:17.557 --> 00:05:21.074
"Bring a torch, Dick," said Long John.

56
00:05:21.075 --> 00:05:30.145
One of the pirates left the log house and returned with a long piece of burning wood.

